# MRL family 8: subjects that must produce NO match.
#
# These are near-miss subjects: one repetition short of a quantifier's own
# minimum, or missing the trailing construct entirely. The interesting
# risk direction is a bound computed too LARGE: that kind of bug prunes
# real matches (turns 'm' into 'n' elsewhere), so a genuine 'n' case here
# is not itself proof of a too-large bound -- but combined with families
# 1-7's positive matches at the true minimum, a too-large bound would show
# up as one of THOSE turning into a wrong 'n', not as a case in this file
# gaining a spurious match. Kept here as the complementary boundary check.


# below the quantifier's own minimum -- no amount of searching finds a valid decomposition.
pattern a{3,6}b
n "aab"
n "b"
n ""

# far below the nested minimum (4).
pattern (a{2,3}){2,3}
n "a"
n ""
n "aaa"

# 2 reps of the SHORT branch -- below the outer quantifier's own minimum (3) even though the short branch is being used.
pattern (?:xxxxx|y){3,4}z
n "yyz"
m "yyyz" 0 4

# one 'b' short of the second quantifier's minimum -- the first quantifier being satisfied does not help.
pattern a{5,10}b{5,10}$
n "aaaaaaaaaabbbb"
m "aaaaaaaaaabbbbb" 0 15
